Ray Kulhanek wrote:
> 
> I agree that ASCII encoding is definitely the way to go if any
> redesign of the savefile is to be done. 

I don't think that a textual data representation would solve the issue.
You'd need some synchronisation points or, as you suggested below with
s-expressions, other meta-structure to obtain the necessary redundancy
to recover from partial losses. For a software process to restore data
it's not relevant whether the contained data is textual or binary. The
textual representation would support manual corrections by humans; but
given how exceptionally rare corruptions occur I think there should be
some additional real gain to implement such a new structure.

Portability of save files would be such a gain. But you cannot compare
games played on different systems because of compile time switches that
affect what is available. So we'd either need some canonical version to
play, or, accept that some components of a game stored on one machine
will be missing on another (and hoping that the game design won't show
any inconsistencies between versions with different features).

> Attempts to make the savefile
> more robust by including offsets and backpointers and so on would just
> create more places for data to become corrupt or out of sync.  But in
> ASCII, it'd be immediately clear where the real data ended and
> the corruption began. And likewise where the corruption ends and good
> data begins again.  Most of the data could probably be represented
> without using binary: objects could use key/value pairs or
> s-expressions; levels could be described in the same format the level
> compiler uses. (I think the level format can also specify monsters
> and objects, but I'm not sure how specific it can be. Might just
> be things like "random h", "random armor")  But it'd probably be
> doable.

Another option (that I suggest every 5 or so years :-) is using ASN.1
(and an appropriate encoding like BER, CER, DER) for the definitions
(and stored representation respectively) of the data.

> The only downside (aside from the time it would take to implement, of
> course) is that savescummers would be able to do things like put an
> ascension kit on the dlvl1 if they wanted to.  Some things could be
> tested for and cause a death by trickery, like if they have dlvl2
> as the deepest level but still have the Amulet, but anything that
> would stop a determined cheater would also stop most genuine attempts
> at recovery.  I don't believe that that by itself is sufficient
> reason not to make the change; people still couldn't cheat on public
> servers, and if they cheat on their home systems, it's their own loss.

As you conclude I also don't consider cheating a problem.

